data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":2552,"item_id":"fc_0c17af8fc60a9be3016adb51c8630c8191a84f65a2be65bcde","output_index":2,"arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_candidate\":2,\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/locals/4/declaration\",\"value\":\"From M003 and the exact M008 formula, for each positive scale r_j tending to zero choose a Nat-indexed cover (U_{j,i}) of E with all ediam(U_{j,i})≤r_j and total q-cost <γ_j, where γ_j>0 tends to zero. The zero-diameter members are subsequently replaced, each by a ball of diameter ≤r_j and added q-cost whose sum is <γ_j; this witness depends on the M003 zero-measure result and M008 cover infimum, not on compactness alone.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/locals/13/declaration\",\"value\":\"For a>0 and δ>0, C_a(A,δ) is the infimum in ENNReal of ∑'_{i:ℕ} ediam(U_i)^a over all Nat-indexed sequences of subsets U_i of the same ambient metric space satisfying A⊆⋃_{i:ℕ}U_i and ediam(U_i)≤δ for every i. Empty members and zero-diameter members are permitted and cost zero because 0^a=0. This is precisely the scale content appearing in M008; covers with only finitely many members are represented by adding empty members.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\",\"value\":\"Let D=dim_H(E), F=E×[0,1]. First show 0≤D≤n<∞. Nonnegativity is part of the critical-exponent definition. For n=0, ℝ⁰ is a singleton and E is that singleton, so D=0. For n>0 compactness H002 implies boundedness, so E lies in a bounded box. For every sufficiently small h>0, subdivide the box into at most C h^(−n) grid cubes, where C is independent of h; each cube has diameter at most √n h. For any fixed m>n the total m-power diameter cost is at most C n^(m/2)h^(m−n), which tends to zero. These are countable covers with scales tending to zero; by the defining scale formula for Hausdorff measure, H^m(E)=0 and thus D≤n. Fix arbitrary q>D; since D≥0 this q is positive. M003, at the NNReal coercion of q, gives H^q(E)=0. For each scale r>0, M008 expresses this measure as the supremum over positive scales of the infimum of cover costs. Since all costs are nonnegative and the supremum is zero, every scale content C_q(E,r) is zero. Hence for any γ>0, by the definition of infimum there is a Nat-indexed r-cover of E of total q-cost <γ; admissible covers exist, for instance a Nat-indexed singleton cover. Choose r_j↓0 with r_j≤1 and γ_j↓0, and choose such covers. Enumerate the nonempty zero-diameter members (if any) as a finite or countable list; in Euclidean space each is a singleton. Replace the k-th by an open ball containing it, of diameter at most r_j and q-power diameter <γ_j2^(−k−1). The total added q-cost is <γ_j. Leave positive-diameter members unchanged and empty members empty. Now every nonempty cover member has positive diameter d_i≤r_j and the total q-cost is <2γ_j. For each such member partition [0,1] into at most ⌈1/d_i⌉+1≤2/d_i closed intervals of length at most d_i. The products U_i×K_{iℓ} cover F and have diameter at most √2d_i by the concatenated metric. The family of pairs (i,ℓ) is countable (a countable union of finite sets), so enumerate it by ℕ and append empty sets if finite. The total (q+1)-cost is at most ∑_i(2/d_i)(√2d_i)^(q+1)=2(√2)^(q+1)∑_i d_i^q<4(√2)^(q+1)γ_j, tending to zero, and the product diameters are at most √2r_j→0. By M008, the scale contents of F at exponent q+1 therefore have supremum zero, so H^(q+1)(F)=0. M012 at NNReal exponent q+1 gives dim_H(F)≤q+1. As this is true for every q>D, density/order continuity gives dim_H(F)≤D+1. For n=0, F is isometric to [0,1]; direct interval covers of mesh tending to zero have total a-cost tending to zero for every a>1, so H^a(F)=0 and M012 gives dim_H(F)≤1=D+1. Isometries preserve ediam, cover conditions and the exact M008 formula.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/1/bindings\",\"value\":\"Instantiate with α=ℝ, its Borel measurable space, β=ℕ, μ=ν=volume.restrict([0,1]), and f(i,t)=ρ_i^s·1_{J_i∩[0,1]}(t).\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/1/hypothesis_checks/0\",\"value\":\"The index ℕ is countable. Each J_i∩[0,1] is Borel in ℝ, so its indicator is measurable; multiplication by constant ρ_i^s preserves measurability and therefore gives AEMeasurability for ν.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/6/bindings\",\"value\":\"Apply on α=ℝ with ν=volume.restrict([0,1]) to the pointwise inequality after multiplying its left side by 1_[0,1]; the measure is concentrated on [0,1], so this is the same ν-almost-everywhere inequality.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/8/hypothesis_checks/0\",\"value\":\"J_i∩[0,1] is Borel, hence measurable in the Borel space ℝ; ρ_i^s is ENNReal, so M010 applies for the restricted measure ν.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/9/bindings\",\"value\":\"Let [a_i,b_i] be the closed interval enclosure, with a_i≤b_i and b_i−a_i≤ρ_i+η_i; instantiate Real.volume_Icc at these endpoints.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/9/hypothesis_checks/0\",\"value\":\"No extra source hypothesis: for arbitrary real endpoints a_i,b_i the theorem states volume([a_i,b_i])=ENNReal.ofReal(b_i−a_i); the construction ensures a_i≤b_i and the displayed length bound.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/text\",\"value\":\"For D>0 fix 0<s<D. For a>0 define C_a(A,δ)=inf{∑'ᵢ ediam(Uᵢ)^a : U:ℕ→Set(X), A⊆⋃ᵢUᵢ and ∀i ediam(Uᵢ)≤δ}. Empty members have diameter zero and hence zero cost. In M008, the supremum over nonempty witnesses is the ordinary power for a nonempty member and zero for the empty member; thus its inner infimum is exactly this content and H^a(A)=sup_{δ>0}C_a(A,δ), with no normalization factor. If δ₁≤δ₂, every δ₁-admissible cover is δ₂-admissible, so C_a(A,δ₁)≥C_a(A,δ₂). The one-sided limit as scale decreases to zero therefore equals this supremum. M001 gives H^s(E)=∞; hence C_s(E,δ)→∞, meaning that for every finite K all sufficiently small positive δ have C_s(E,δ)>K. Fix δ>0 and any countable δ-cover (Vᵢ) of F. Set ρᵢ=ediam(Vᵢ), Aᵢ=π₁(Vᵢ), Bᵢ=π₂(Vᵢ). Coordinate projections are 1-Lipschitz in the concatenated Euclidean metric, so diam(Aᵢ),diam(Bᵢ)≤ρᵢ≤δ. Given ε>0 set ηᵢ=ε2^(−i−1)/(1+ρᵢ^s)>0. Each Bᵢ is bounded; extending the interval between its infimum and supremum by total width ηᵢ gives an open interval Jᵢ⊇Bᵢ with length ≤ρᵢ+ηᵢ. For every t∈[0,1] and x∈E, (x,t) lies in some Vᵢ. Thus x∈Aᵢ and t∈Bᵢ⊆Jᵢ; so the Aᵢ with t∈Jᵢ cover E at scale δ. By the definition of the infimum, C_s(E,δ)≤∑'ᵢ1_{Jᵢ∩[0,1]}(t)ρᵢ^s pointwise for t∈[0,1]. Let ν=volume.restrict([0,1]) on the ambient Borel space ℝ. For integration on all ℝ, multiply the left side by 1_[0,1]; outside [0,1] the left side is zero, and ν is supported on [0,1], so the inequality holds ν-almost everywhere. Each Jᵢ∩[0,1] is Borel, hence its indicator multiplied by ρᵢ^s is measurable and AEMeasurable for ν. The total ambient mass is ν(ℝ)=volume([0,1])=ofReal(1−0)=1 by the restriction definition and M011; thus ν is finite, and in particular ν([0,1])=1. Integrate by M009 and use M002 to interchange the countable sum and lintegral; its hypotheses hold because the index is ℕ and every summand is AEMeasurable. By M010 each integral equals ρᵢ^sν(Jᵢ∩[0,1]). For S=Jᵢ∩[0,1], the restriction definition gives ν(S)=volume(S∩[0,1])=volume(S). Choose a closed interval [aᵢ,bᵢ] containing S with aᵢ≤bᵢ and bᵢ−aᵢ≤ρᵢ+ηᵢ. M013, measure monotonicity, gives volume(S)≤volume([aᵢ,bᵢ]); M011 evaluates the latter as ofReal(bᵢ−aᵢ)≤ofReal(ρᵢ+ηᵢ). Consequently C_s(E,δ)≤∑'ᵢρᵢ^(s+1)+∑'ᵢηᵢρᵢ^s≤∑'ᵢρᵢ^(s+1)+ε, since ηᵢρᵢ^s≤ε2^(−i−1) and ∑ᵢ2^(−i−1)=1. The cover is fixed and ε arbitrary: if its (s+1)-cost is finite, taking ε↓0 proves C_s(E,δ)≤that cost; if it is infinite the bound is immediate. Since this holds for every cover, taking the infimum gives C_s(E,δ)≤C_(s+1)(F,δ). As δ decreases to zero the left side diverges, so the right side diverges too; M008 gives H^(s+1)(F)=∞. Apply M001 with NNReal exponent s+1 to obtain s+1≤dim_H(F). This is true for all 0<s<D, so density of the reals implies D+1≤dim_H(F). At each use s is coerced to NNReal for dimension theorems and the corresponding real exponent in M008.\"}]}}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":2553,"output_index":2,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_0c17af8fc60a9be3016adb51c8630c8191a84f65a2be65bcde","call_id":"call_32fd171632dd4d96b685436f3853f802","name":"check_draft","arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_candidate\":2,\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/locals/4/declaration\",\"value\":\"From M003 and the exact M008 formula, for each positive scale r_j tending to zero choose a Nat-indexed cover (U_{j,i}) of E with all ediam(U_{j,i})≤r_j and total q-cost <γ_j, where γ_j>0 tends to zero. The zero-diameter members are subsequently replaced, each by a ball of diameter ≤r_j and added q-cost whose sum is <γ_j; this witness depends on the M003 zero-measure result and M008 cover infimum, not on compactness alone.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/locals/13/declaration\",\"value\":\"For a>0 and δ>0, C_a(A,δ) is the infimum in ENNReal of ∑'_{i:ℕ} ediam(U_i)^a over all Nat-indexed sequences of subsets U_i of the same ambient metric space satisfying A⊆⋃_{i:ℕ}U_i and ediam(U_i)≤δ for every i. Empty members and zero-diameter members are permitted and cost zero because 0^a=0. This is precisely the scale content appearing in M008; covers with only finitely many members are represented by adding empty members.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\",\"value\":\"Let D=dim_H(E), F=E×[0,1]. First show 0≤D≤n<∞. Nonnegativity is part of the critical-exponent definition. For n=0, ℝ⁰ is a singleton and E is that singleton, so D=0. For n>0 compactness H002 implies boundedness, so E lies in a bounded box. For every sufficiently small h>0, subdivide the box into at most C h^(−n) grid cubes, where C is independent of h; each cube has diameter at most √n h. For any fixed m>n the total m-power diameter cost is at most C n^(m/2)h^(m−n), which tends to zero. These are countable covers with scales tending to zero; by the defining scale formula for Hausdorff measure, H^m(E)=0 and thus D≤n. Fix arbitrary q>D; since D≥0 this q is positive. M003, at the NNReal coercion of q, gives H^q(E)=0. For each scale r>0, M008 expresses this measure as the supremum over positive scales of the infimum of cover costs. Since all costs are nonnegative and the supremum is zero, every scale content C_q(E,r) is zero. Hence for any γ>0, by the definition of infimum there is a Nat-indexed r-cover of E of total q-cost <γ; admissible covers exist, for instance a Nat-indexed singleton cover. Choose r_j↓0 with r_j≤1 and γ_j↓0, and choose such covers. Enumerate the nonempty zero-diameter members (if any) as a finite or countable list; in Euclidean space each is a singleton. Replace the k-th by an open ball containing it, of diameter at most r_j and q-power diameter <γ_j2^(−k−1). The total added q-cost is <γ_j. Leave positive-diameter members unchanged and empty members empty. Now every nonempty cover member has positive diameter d_i≤r_j and the total q-cost is <2γ_j. For each such member partition [0,1] into at most ⌈1/d_i⌉+1≤2/d_i closed intervals of length at most d_i. The products U_i×K_{iℓ} cover F and have diameter at most √2d_i by the concatenated metric. The family of pairs (i,ℓ) is countable (a countable union of finite sets), so enumerate it by ℕ and append empty sets if finite. The total (q+1)-cost is at most ∑_i(2/d_i)(√2d_i)^(q+1)=2(√2)^(q+1)∑_i d_i^q<4(√2)^(q+1)γ_j, tending to zero, and the product diameters are at most √2r_j→0. By M008, the scale contents of F at exponent q+1 therefore have supremum zero, so H^(q+1)(F)=0. M012 at NNReal exponent q+1 gives dim_H(F)≤q+1. As this is true for every q>D, density/order continuity gives dim_H(F)≤D+1. For n=0, F is isometric to [0,1]; direct interval covers of mesh tending to zero have total a-cost tending to zero for every a>1, so H^a(F)=0 and M012 gives dim_H(F)≤1=D+1. Isometries preserve ediam, cover conditions and the exact M008 formula.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/1/bindings\",\"value\":\"Instantiate with α=ℝ, its Borel measurable space, β=ℕ, μ=ν=volume.restrict([0,1]), and f(i,t)=ρ_i^s·1_{J_i∩[0,1]}(t).\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/1/hypothesis_checks/0\",\"value\":\"The index ℕ is countable. Each J_i∩[0,1] is Borel in ℝ, so its indicator is measurable; multiplication by constant ρ_i^s preserves measurability and therefore gives AEMeasurability for ν.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/6/bindings\",\"value\":\"Apply on α=ℝ with ν=volume.restrict([0,1]) to the pointwise inequality after multiplying its left side by 1_[0,1]; the measure is concentrated on [0,1], so this is the same ν-almost-everywhere inequality.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/8/hypothesis_checks/0\",\"value\":\"J_i∩[0,1] is Borel, hence measurable in the Borel space ℝ; ρ_i^s is ENNReal, so M010 applies for the restricted measure ν.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/9/bindings\",\"value\":\"Let [a_i,b_i] be the closed interval enclosure, with a_i≤b_i and b_i−a_i≤ρ_i+η_i; instantiate Real.volume_Icc at these endpoints.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/9/hypothesis_checks/0\",\"value\":\"No extra source hypothesis: for arbitrary real endpoints a_i,b_i the theorem states volume([a_i,b_i])=ENNReal.ofReal(b_i−a_i); the construction ensures a_i≤b_i and the displayed length bound.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/text\",\"value\":\"For D>0 fix 0<s<D. For a>0 define C_a(A,δ)=inf{∑'ᵢ ediam(Uᵢ)^a : U:ℕ→Set(X), A⊆⋃ᵢUᵢ and ∀i ediam(Uᵢ)≤δ}. Empty members have diameter zero and hence zero cost. In M008, the supremum over nonempty witnesses is the ordinary power for a nonempty member and zero for the empty member; thus its inner infimum is exactly this content and H^a(A)=sup_{δ>0}C_a(A,δ), with no normalization factor. If δ₁≤δ₂, every δ₁-admissible cover is δ₂-admissible, so C_a(A,δ₁)≥C_a(A,δ₂). The one-sided limit as scale decreases to zero therefore equals this supremum. M001 gives H^s(E)=∞; hence C_s(E,δ)→∞, meaning that for every finite K all sufficiently small positive δ have C_s(E,δ)>K. Fix δ>0 and any countable δ-cover (Vᵢ) of F. Set ρᵢ=ediam(Vᵢ), Aᵢ=π₁(Vᵢ), Bᵢ=π₂(Vᵢ). Coordinate projections are 1-Lipschitz in the concatenated Euclidean metric, so diam(Aᵢ),diam(Bᵢ)≤ρᵢ≤δ. Given ε>0 set ηᵢ=ε2^(−i−1)/(1+ρᵢ^s)>0. Each Bᵢ is bounded; extending the interval between its infimum and supremum by total width ηᵢ gives an open interval Jᵢ⊇Bᵢ with length ≤ρᵢ+ηᵢ. For every t∈[0,1] and x∈E, (x,t) lies in some Vᵢ. Thus x∈Aᵢ and t∈Bᵢ⊆Jᵢ; so the Aᵢ with t∈Jᵢ cover E at scale δ. By the definition of the infimum, C_s(E,δ)≤∑'ᵢ1_{Jᵢ∩[0,1]}(t)ρᵢ^s pointwise for t∈[0,1]. Let ν=volume.restrict([0,1]) on the ambient Borel space ℝ. For integration on all ℝ, multiply the left side by 1_[0,1]; outside [0,1] the left side is zero, and ν is supported on [0,1], so the inequality holds ν-almost everywhere. Each Jᵢ∩[0,1] is Borel, hence its indicator multiplied by ρᵢ^s is measurable and AEMeasurable for ν. The total ambient mass is ν(ℝ)=volume([0,1])=ofReal(1−0)=1 by the restriction definition and M011; thus ν is finite, and in particular ν([0,1])=1. Integrate by M009 and use M002 to interchange the countable sum and lintegral; its hypotheses hold because the index is ℕ and every summand is AEMeasurable. By M010 each integral equals ρᵢ^sν(Jᵢ∩[0,1]). For S=Jᵢ∩[0,1], the restriction definition gives ν(S)=volume(S∩[0,1])=volume(S). Choose a closed interval [aᵢ,bᵢ] containing S with aᵢ≤bᵢ and bᵢ−aᵢ≤ρᵢ+ηᵢ. M013, measure monotonicity, gives volume(S)≤volume([aᵢ,bᵢ]); M011 evaluates the latter as ofReal(bᵢ−aᵢ)≤ofReal(ρᵢ+ηᵢ). Consequently C_s(E,δ)≤∑'ᵢρᵢ^(s+1)+∑'ᵢηᵢρᵢ^s≤∑'ᵢρᵢ^(s+1)+ε, since ηᵢρᵢ^s≤ε2^(−i−1) and ∑ᵢ2^(−i−1)=1. The cover is fixed and ε arbitrary: if its (s+1)-cost is finite, taking ε↓0 proves C_s(E,δ)≤that cost; if it is infinite the bound is immediate. Since this holds for every cover, taking the infimum gives C_s(E,δ)≤C_(s+1)(F,δ). As δ decreases to zero the left side diverges, so the right side diverges too; M008 gives H^(s+1)(F)=∞. Apply M001 with NNReal exponent s+1 to obtain s+1≤dim_H(F). This is true for all 0<s<D, so density of the reals implies D+1≤dim_H(F). At each use s is coerced to NNReal for dimension theorems and the corresponding real exponent in M008.\"}]}}","status":"completed"}}

